WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.120
Film Courage:&nbsp; What do you say to screenwriters
that are afraid someone will steal either

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.549
their idea or their finished script?

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:09.170
Lee Jessup:&nbsp; This is really so unsexy.&nbsp;

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.430
Get over it!&nbsp;

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.550
There really is nothing for the industry to
gain from stealing work.&nbsp;

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.120
If you’re a brand new writer and you wrote
something that is the greatest idea ever,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.369
the industry is much better off (you’re
not a WGA member) buying that script from

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:29.880
me for $25,000, $30,000, maybe $50,000 and
doing whatever they want with it versus potentially

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.050
getting tied up in lawsuits.&nbsp;

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.499
It really doesn’t pay anybody to steal work
in this industry.&nbsp;

00:00:36.499 --> 00:00:39.659
You can’t come at the industry from a place
of mistrust.&nbsp;

00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:44.020
You can’t come at agents and managers saying
“Well, I’ll send you my work but I’m

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.300
really nervous because I’ve heard about
how scripts get stolen” because then you’re

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.120
coming at it from a place of “I” automatically
do not trust you [i.e., The Industry].&nbsp;

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.120
I’m the writer.&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.559
You’re going to victimize me.&nbsp;

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:56.729
Nobody wants to work with that.&nbsp;

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:57.789
Nobody wants to deal with that.&nbsp;

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:02.499
The reality is when Bridesmaids came out,
I can’t tell you how many writers came to

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:03.499
me and said “Oh my God!&nbsp;

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:04.519
I wrote that exact same script.”

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.350
There are ideas that are zietgeistsy.&nbsp;

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:12.500
There are ideas that are in the moment and
those ideas tend to manifest in many different

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.500
places.&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.750
But it really does not serve you in any way
to think that the industry will steal your

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.750
work.&nbsp;

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.040
Your job with your script that you have (if
you’re writing outside of the industry and

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.950
in all likelihood if you have a feature script),
the job of that feature script is to get you

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.820
an agent, to get you a manager.&nbsp;

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:29.820
That is it’s job.&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.969
It likely won’t sell as it is to the industry.&nbsp;

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:38.070
We are in this industry in 2016, we are likely
to sell less than a hundred specs.&nbsp;

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.530
That is not a big number.&nbsp;

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:44.179
If you’re aiming for that bullseye, that
is a narrow, narrow, narrow bullseye.&nbsp;

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:47.810
You want to use that script to get you an
agent, to get you a manager, to introduce

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.509
you to the industry, to get on the prestige
list, that’s what you want this script to

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:52.509
do for you.&nbsp;

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:55.649
In order to do that you have to expose the
work.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:02:00.340
Get over any idea that somebody will steal
your script because that’s just not going

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.340
to happen.&nbsp;

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:09.910
And on the rare, rare, rare occasions that
it has, the writer usually got their own success

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:10.910
in their own way.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.790
Film Courage:&nbsp; Well, let’s continue with
the topic of mistrust.&nbsp;

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:20.020
On your screenwriting blog for your website
LeeJessup.com you have a post entitled ‘Screenwriters

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.460
Leave Your Mistrust at the Door.”

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.460
Lee Jessup:&nbsp; Yes.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.920
Film Courage:&nbsp; I know some of us are way
more trusting than others.&nbsp;

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.620
Some of us are maybe more on the naïve scale.&nbsp;

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.690
You talked about a lot of things that many
people would be afraid of like They Want Me

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:33.690
to Sign a Release Form?&nbsp;

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:34.710
I mean, that is scary.&nbsp;

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.420
That’s a little scary.&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.860
Or I think we’re touching on the same thing
as before “Somebody Has the Same Concept

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:41.860
of Mine.&nbsp;

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.860
Somebody Stole My Idea.”&nbsp;

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.590
“Wouldn’t They Rather Cheat Me Than Pay
Me?”&nbsp;

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:51.630
So...how do you work with someone who is already
coming in with those set of fears and make

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.830
them so they are a little more workable, but
not naïve and not a doormat?

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:02.020
Lee Jessup:&nbsp; I always tell my writers that
I’m the safe space so you can let out all

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.280
of your neuroses and I’ll put you in your
place.&nbsp;

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:11.010
I actually wrote that particular blog post
after a writer of mine had finished a script.&nbsp;

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:20.660
The script had been entered into one contest,
hadn’t placed and another script with eerie

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.830
similarities sold.&nbsp;

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.440
Eerie, eerie, eerie similarities.&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.760
And the writer came to me and said “What
Am I To Make of This?”&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.650
And we had to kind of sit back and say “O.K.
here is where the script was.&nbsp;

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.810
Here is the writer who just sold a script.&nbsp;

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.970
There is no... as much as I understand there
are eerie similarities, let’s talk through

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:50.150
what the probability would be of this writer,
an established writer with a summer blockbuster

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.150
would have of stealing your script.&nbsp;

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.150
Let’s talk through it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.200
I always tell me writers “I’m a Safe Space.&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.200
We Need to Talk About It.”&nbsp;

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:00.970
But...at some point a writer either wises
up or not.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.931
I work with a writer that I no longer work
with who refused to pitch any ideas, talk

00:04:05.931 --> 00:04:11.260
log lines, or anything as such in a public
environment.&nbsp;

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.460
So in a coffee shop, in a bar, which is where
a lot of industry meetings take place mind

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:20.290
you, the writer refused to do it because somebody
would listen and somebody would steal this

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.750
idea and it became uncomfortable.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.810
At some point I had to say to the writer “Listen,
you’re going to have managers meeting with

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:27.810
you.”&nbsp;

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.730
There was a manager who wanted to meet with
that writer in a coffee shop.&nbsp;

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.540
So the manager is going to ask you “What
else do you have?”&nbsp;

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.640
What are you going to say?&nbsp;

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.750
“I can’t tell you here.&nbsp;

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:35.750
I’ll send you an email.”&nbsp;

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.460
It doesn’t work that way.&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.340
Even if you have it innately, you have to
set it aside.&nbsp;

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.540
You have to.&nbsp;

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:49.470
Because this industry works the way that it
does with the idea of trust and respecting

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.310
the creative mind, the creative brain, the
originator of content.&nbsp;

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:58.740
If you originated a great idea, a great script,
a great story, why would would I steal it

00:04:58.740 --> 00:04:59.740
from you?&nbsp;

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.360
Why wouldn’t I just take the thing that
is already done and do something with it?&nbsp;

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.940
Wouldn’t that make more sense for me?&nbsp;

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:09.050
If I’m a producer, an executive, why would
I steal it, get somebody else to write it,

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:15.300
potentially for more money because they are
already well known, either they executed well

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.530
or they don’t .&nbsp; Maybe they don’t.&nbsp;

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.920
Or somebody else does the same thing to you
before my script is done, why wouldn’t I

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.660
just pick it up from you, the creator of content
who came up with this great concept, what

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.040
incentive do I (an executive) have to steal
it from you?&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.860
And that is what a lot of writers have to
(despite how frustrating it can get) look

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:34.860
at.&nbsp;

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.120
And that’s ultimately what that writer and
myself have to look at when a script with

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.400
eerie similarities to this individual’s
sold.&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.950
There was no way for the manager who sold
that script to get that script.&nbsp;

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.010
There was no way that an industry executive
had read that script.&nbsp;

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.590
It has been submitted to one place, three
months before.&nbsp;

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:59.120
There was just no reality in which the script
was stolen or rewritten.&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.460
What the writer had to acknowledge was that
ultimately if I’m the manager behind the

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:09.990
script sale and let’s assume I’ve got
a completed script that is great which I think

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:15.669
I can sell and I have this writer who just
had a box office success, what is my best

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:16.919
way of making the most money?&nbsp;

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:21.490
My best way of making the most money is getting
the one script that is ready to sell and selling

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.650
it and booking my writer who just had a big
box-office success on a writing assignment

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.050
and making more money.&nbsp;

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:32.810
Why limit the ways in which I’m making money
by having the writer then take the script

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:38.270
and try to make money off of it rather than
monetize both?

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.830
So those are the equations that writers have
to look at.&nbsp;

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:44.220
Is it really in the industry’s best interest
to steal content?&nbsp;

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:45.372
I really don’t find that it is.

00:06:45.372 --> 00:06:46.372
QUESTION TO THE VIEWERS:&nbsp; Do you agree?&nbsp;

00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:46.380
What is your stance on Hollywood stealing
ideas?

